
"Worst Case" Benchmark Series run 6-May-1990 under Genera 7.2.2 on a
Symbolics 3620 with 3M Words of RAM and 10M Bytes of "Swapping" Space.

In this test a string is formed by repeating the alphabet, in ascending
order, once for each element of the alphabet.  Therefore the "magnitude"
of the input string is equal to the square of the alphabet length.

The "Worst Case" test then consists of finding the Longest Common Sequence
between this string, and its reversal.


Alphabet	 Pairs		"Fast"		"Fast"		"Hybrid"     Previous
 Length		Matched		 Pairs		 Time		  Time	       Time
--------	-------		------		------		--------     --------
   25		   49		15,025		   5.1		    13.1         17.4


   50		   99		122,550		  49		    91	        122
								 (1:31)       (2:02)

  100		  199		990,100		 475		   705	        883
					       (7:55)	        (11:45)      (14:43)

  200		  399	       7,960,200       5,390		 5,813	      6,683
					    (1:29:50)	      (1:36:53)	   (1:51:23)

  250		  499	      15,562,750      11,965		11,515	     13,098
					    (3:19:25)	      (3:11:55)	   (3:38:18)


Cross-Over Search
-----------------
Non-symmetrized Hybrid on 250
took: 13,098 seconds (3:38:18).

#'>= Symmetrized Hybrid on 250
took: 11,515 seconds (3:11:55).

Cross-over Occurred!  "Fast" on 250
took: 11,965 seconds (3:19:25).


The following equations fit the observed data well:

    Fast Time	= (/ (expt alphabet-length 3.37) 10600)

   Hybrid Time	= (/ (expt alphabet-length 2.95) 1080)

 [Previous Time	= (/ (expt alphabet-length 2.88) 620)]


These indicate that "cross over" should occur somewhere
in the vicinity of an alphabet length of 230 [previously 340].

At this point the time required would be about 8,585 [previously 29,520] seconds,
or 2:23:05 [previously 8:12:00].  459 [previously 679] Pairs would be matched,
and the "Fast" algolrithm would CONS 12,114,330 [previously 39,188,740] Pairs in
the process of doing so.

The "record sequences" would be 52,900 [previously 115,600] elements in length.
This would be the equivalent of 840 [previously 1,835], full, typewritten pages.

Note that the cross-over point will be further out for any pair of strings not
producing "worst case" behaviour (in the general case.)

The tests below, for example, show the results of allowing strings
of the same magnitude (the square of the alphabet length) to be
formed out of randomly selected letters (drawn from an alphabet of
the indicated length).  On average, the "Fast" Times have increased
by 25% while the "Hybrid" Times have increased by 40%.


Alphabet	 Pairs		"Fast"		"Fast"		Previous
 Length		Matched		 Pairs		 Time		  Time
--------	-------		------		------		--------
   25		  199		 9,856		   6.3		   27.3


   50	     603 (vs. 601)	86,105		  61.6		  171.3
								(2:51.3)

  100	   1,819 (vs. 1,786)   779,914		 612		 1183.3
					      (10:12)	       (19:43.3)


These data suggest that the "Hybrid" algorithm would have to make a
"constant" performance gain on the "Fast" algorithm of at least 2X,
in order to justify conversion to the "Hybrid" algorithm.

Even though 40M Words of Garbage Generation is not an unreasonable burden
on existing systems, higher cross-over points introduce the possibility
that the "Hybrid" algorithm might be favored due to the linearity of its
generation of garbage.


PROTOTYPE DESCRIPTION FOR ALGORITHM.TEXT
========================================
A somewhat more elaborate, recursive, version of the preceeding algorithm
(which will not be developed here) confines its "new_node" memory to a
linear O(m + n) bound.  As presented, however, the time it requires is of
order O(mn).  It turns out this algorithm can be combined with the algorithm
presented below to lower the time requirement to also be O((r + s) log s).
Unfortunately, this hybrid algorithm starts out with a substantially higher
"constant level" of overhead.

Because of its linear space dependency, the hybrid algorithm might be
useful in an applications where an enormously long worst case pair of
sequences causes the algorithm presented below to allocate an unreasonably
large amount of space.  In forseeable practical applications, however,
the algorithm presented here will retain a substantial speed advantage.

  Worst case conditions are achieved when the two sequences are comprised
  of sigma cycles of an alphabet of size sigma.  That is, when both the
  alphabet size and the number of cycles are near the square root of the
  sequence length.  Comparing such a sequence with its own reflection
  (or reversal) then results in the "worst case conditions".

"Worst Case" Benchmark Series run 6-May-1990 under Genera 7.2.2 on a
Symbolics 3620 with 3M Words of RAM and 10M Bytes of "Swapping" Space.

In this test a string is formed by repeating the alphabet, in ascending
order, once for each element of the alphabet.  Therefore the "magnitude"
of the input string is equal to the square of the alphabet length.

The "Worst Case" test then consists of finding the Longest Common Sequence
between this string, and its reversal.


